I think there could be a number of differences among ordinations among different softwares. PLINK only uses biallelic loci where adegenet has the potential to use multi-allelic loci (although you did state you only used biallelic loci). I believe adegenet will use column means (locus means) to impute missing data. PLINK may use some other method, or just omit loci with missing data. Zhian makes a good point about centering and scaling, which is common with morphological and ecological data but probably isn't necessary if you're using biallelic loci. And the distance that you use to compare the samples may be different in PLINK than adegent. There are many options for distances in R. I think you'd have to account for all of that in order to make a comparison.

Good luck!
Brian

